import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const steps=[["Input","One keyword or short question, 2–100 characters. Country and language are optional. URLs, files and additional fields are rejected."],["Human check","Cloudflare Turnstile must pass before the request can reserve budget or call a model."],["One bounded run","The server asks an allowlisted OpenAI model through OpenRouter to use native web search. It permits at most eight tool calls and stops after 12 seconds."],["Observed queries","The parser reads only query strings inside provider-returned search-action objects. If no query field exists, the run fails with “provider query trace unavailable.”"],["Visible sources","The parser collects cited or returned source URLs from the same response. A source is evidence of visibility in that run, not an endorsement or ranking."],["No raw storage","The keyword and provider response are processed in memory and not stored by ai-fanout.com. The service keeps only salted usage hashes, versions, status, cost and timing."],["Hard limits","One hashed IP bucket gets five attempts per 24 hours; the site gets 40 per UTC day. Each request reserves up to €0.15 inside €25 soft and €30 hard monthly limits."],["Versioned result","Every successful result names the model, provider, observation time, tool version and evidence status so it can be interpreted as one dated observation."]];
---
<BaseLayout title="How the AI query fanout tool works" canonicalPath="/methodology" alternatePath="/de/methode" description="How the free tool obtains provider-exposed OpenAI web-search queries and sources, with strict privacy, quota and evidence limits."><header class="page-head"><h1>How the free tool works</h1><div class="page-head-copy"><p>A short input goes in. Provider-exposed web searches and source URLs come back. Nothing missing is guessed.</p><p class="meta-line">Tool observed-query-tool/1.0.0 · OpenAI via OpenRouter · owner Matthias Ramahi</p></div></header><div class="document-layout"><aside class="document-aside"><dl><div><dt>Availability</dt><dd>Not open yet</dd></div><div><dt>Provider route</dt><dd>OpenAI via OpenRouter</dd></div><div><dt>Search-call limit</dt><dd>8</dd></div><div><dt>Time limit</dt><dd>12 seconds</dd></div></dl></aside><article class="document-body"><h2>One run, clearly labelled.</h2><p class="document-intro">This is not a simulation of hidden ChatGPT behaviour. It is an inspection of web-search actions that an API response explicitly returns for one bounded run.</p><ol class="controls">{steps.map(([title,copy],index)=><li><span>{String(index+1).padStart(2,"0")}</span><div><h3>{title}</h3><p>{copy}</p></div></li>)}</ol><section><h2>Why OpenRouter does not automatically prove query access</h2><p>OpenRouter documents native web search and its Responses endpoint. It does not guarantee that every routed model and response shape will preserve exact search-query strings. The public endpoint therefore remains closed until a live, owner-authorized fixture confirms that the selected OpenAI route returns those strings. If it does not, the product must use a route that does or narrow its promise.</p></section><section><h2>What the result can and cannot tell you</h2><p>It can document the queries and URLs exposed in one API response. It cannot reveal chain of thought, private retrieval traces, every search the provider may have performed, search volume, rankings, or what another model would do.</p></section><section><h2>Responsibility</h2><p>Matthias Ramahi owns protocol versions, corrections, reviewer status and the monthly cost envelope. No independent review or provider benchmark is claimed. Public examples require a date, exact input, model, route and evidence label.</p></section></article></div></BaseLayout>
